1 LIBRARY

   Creates, changes, lists, and extracts from VAX/VMS libraries, as
   VMS's LIBRARIAN does: object libraries (.OLB), which LINK searches for
   modules; macro libraries (.MLB), which MACRO searches for macros;
   shareable image libraries, which LINK searches for images; text
   libraries (.TLB), which FORTRAN's INCLUDE reads; and help libraries
   (.HLB), which HELP reads.  govax writes libraries as LIBRARIAN lays
   them out, so a library made here works on VMS, and VMS's work here.

   Format

     LIBRARY library-file [input-file[,...]]

2 Parameters

 library-file

   The library.  Without a file type it is .OLB, .MLB with /MACRO, .TLB
   with /TEXT, or .HLB with /HELP.  It can be a host file or a file on a
   mounted volume, by MACRO's rules (see MACRO Parameter).

 input-file[,...]

   Files whose modules go into the library:

     - macro source (.MAR, the default type for a macro library), a
       module for each macro;
     - object files (.OBJ, the default for an object library), a module
       for each object module;
     - shareable images (.EXE, the default for a shareable image
       library), a module each, holding the image's name, ident, and
       universal symbols;
     - text files (.TXT, the default for a text library), a module each,
       of the file's records, named for the file or by /MODULE;
     - help files (.HLP, the default for a help library), a module for
       each level-1 key line ("1 KEY").

   An input file may have wildcards: each file it matches is an input,
   in the directory's order.  A bare name is found beside the input
   before it.  With input files and no operation named, their modules
   replace any of the same names, as LIBRARIAN's default /REPLACE does.

2 Description

   LIBRARIAN changes a library in place; LIBRARY builds the whole
   library and writes it only when every step has succeeded, so an error
   leaves the library as it was (but a help file's bad key line, as on
   VMS, ends only that file's reading).  A changed library keeps its name
   and version, and no update history.  /COMPRESS and /DATA aren't
   implemented.

   From the host shell, "govax library library-file [input-file...]"
   runs this command, with --create, --insert, --replace, --delete,
   --extract, --output, --list, --list-file, --full, --names, --macro,
   --object, --share, --text, --no-squeeze, --selective-search, and
   --log.

2 Qualifiers
/CREATE[=(option:n[,...])]

   Creates a new library (a new version on a volume), then inserts any
   input files' modules.  The options are BLOCKS (which has no effect),
   GLOBALS, HISTORY, KEYSIZE, and MODULES.

/DELETE=(module[,...])

   Deletes modules.  Names may hold the wildcards * and %.  Deletions
   are done before any insertions.

/EXTRACT=(module[,...])

   Copies modules out of the library, to the file /OUTPUT names (by
   default, the library's name with the type .MAR, .OBJ, .TXT, or .HLP).
   Names may hold * and %.  /OUTPUT=SYS$OUTPUT shows a text, macro, or
   help module.

/FULL

   With /LIST, adds each module's ident, insertion time, and number of
   global symbols.

/HELP

   A new library is a help library.

/HOST

   After the library or an input file, makes it a host path, whatever
   it looks like.

/INSERT

   Inserts the input files' modules.  A module already in the library is
   a warning, and is left as it is (in a text library, an error that
   ends the command).

/LIST[=filespec]

   Lists the library: its header, then its modules.  Without a file
   name, the listing goes to the terminal; a file's default type is
   .LIS.

/LOG

   Reports each module inserted, replaced, or deleted.

/MACRO

   A new library is a macro library.

/MODULE=name

   After a text file, names its module; by default the module is named
   for the file.

/NAMES

   With /LIST, adds each object module's global symbols.

/OBJECT

   A new library is an object library (the default).

/OUTPUT=filespec

   Where /EXTRACT writes the modules.

/REPLACE

   Replaces modules with the input files' ones of the same names,
   inserting the rest (the default).

/SELECTIVE_SEARCH

   Marks inserted object modules for selective search: LINK takes from
   them only symbols already referred to.

/SHARE

   A new library is a shareable image library, which LINK searches for
   the images that define symbols.

/[NO]SQUEEZE

   /SQUEEZE, the default, removes macros' comments and trailing blanks,
   as LIBRARIAN does, except on .MACRO, .ERROR, .WARN, .PRINT, and .IIF
   lines.  /NOSQUEEZE keeps them.

/TEXT

   A new library is a text library.

/WIDTH=n

   The width of a /NAMES listing (80 at the terminal, 132 in a file).

2 Examples

   1.$ LIBRARY/CREATE/MACRO MYMACROS MYMACROS.MAR
     $ MACRO PROG /LIBRARY=(MYMACROS)

     Makes a macro library, and assembles a program that uses it.

   2.$ LIBRARY/CREATE DUA1:[LIB]SUBS SUB1,SUB2
     $ LINK MAIN,DUA1:[LIB]SUBS/LIBRARY

     Makes an object library, and links a program against it.

   3.$ LIBRARY/LIST/FULL/NAMES DUA1:[LIB]SUBS

     Lists the library's modules and their symbols.

   4.$ LIBRARY/EXTRACT=(ADD*)/OUTPUT="adds.mar" MYMACROS

     Copies the macros whose names start with ADD to a host file.

   5.$ LIBRARY/CREATE/SHARE DUA1:[LIB]MYSHR SHR1.EXE,SHR2.EXE
     $ DEFINE LNK$LIBRARY DUA1:[LIB]MYSHR.OLB
     $ LINK MAIN

     Makes a shareable image library, which LINK then searches.

   6.$ LIBRARY/CREATE/TEXT MYDEFS LIMITS.TXT,TIMES.TXT/MODULE=$TIMES

     Makes a text library of two modules, LIMITS and $TIMES.

   7.$ LIBRARY/CREATE/HELP MYHELP TOOLS.HLP,NOTES.HLP

     Makes a help library.
